1
00:00:00,250 --> 00:00:02,993
Imagine a day when your phone does not ring once.

2
00:00:03,793 --> 00:00:08,704
No alarm. No call from work. No message reminding you what you owe someone.

3
00:00:09,504 --> 00:00:14,049
And on that same day, before noon, money is deposited into your account.

4
00:00:14,849 --> 00:00:18,362
Notice this — the phone buzzes exactly once that day,

5
00:00:18,362 --> 00:00:21,014
and that one buzz is the money arriving.

6
00:00:21,814 --> 00:00:25,001
You did not get rich. You reached one specific number.

7
00:00:25,801 --> 00:00:30,712
That number has one job: to quietly pay your groceries, your gas, and your rent.

8
00:00:31,512 --> 00:00:34,790
Everyone asks how much you need invested to live off dividends,

9
00:00:34,790 --> 00:00:37,599
and the honest answer costs more than the popular one.

10
00:00:38,399 --> 00:00:41,601
In this video that number is built one rung at a time,

11
00:00:41,601 --> 00:00:45,870
starting with the smallest, and one condition rides along on every rung.

12
00:00:46,670 --> 00:00:51,032
Start with the rate, because a number without a rate is a wish with a comma in it.

13
00:00:51,832 --> 00:00:55,649
Think of it this way — you filled a tank slowly, over years,

14
00:00:55,649 --> 00:00:58,606
and the only question that matters is how much

15
00:00:58,606 --> 00:01:01,628
you can draw out each year without emptying it.

16
00:01:02,428 --> 00:01:05,828
That question has a number, and the number is four percent —

17
00:01:05,828 --> 00:01:09,795
but understand where it comes from, because four percent is not a law.

18
00:01:10,594 --> 00:01:13,650
It is two papers, and both of them have names and dates.

19
00:01:14,451 --> 00:01:18,734
William Bengen published the first one in the Journal of Financial Planning,

20
00:01:18,734 --> 00:01:20,538
in October nineteen ninety-four.

21
00:01:21,337 --> 00:01:24,751
He tested a portfolio split half in stocks and half in bonds,

22
00:01:24,751 --> 00:01:27,606
across retirements starting in nineteen twenty-six.

23
00:01:28,407 --> 00:01:31,461
Four years later, three Trinity University professors ran

24
00:01:31,461 --> 00:01:34,676
it again and published it in February nineteen ninety-eight.

25
00:01:35,476 --> 00:01:37,201
Across thirty-year retirements,

26
00:01:37,201 --> 00:01:41,040
four percent survived ninety-five percent of the periods they tested.

27
00:01:41,840 --> 00:01:44,527
So four percent is the working number here,

28
00:01:44,527 --> 00:01:48,527
and it is a finding with a date, not a promise about your money.

29
00:01:49,328 --> 00:01:53,407
Now watch what it buys. Take a yearly bill, divide it by four percent,

30
00:01:53,407 --> 00:01:56,146
and that is the money the bill needs behind it.

31
00:01:56,946 --> 00:01:59,746
The Labor Department says the average household spent ten

32
00:01:59,746 --> 00:02:02,693
thousand one hundred sixty-nine dollars on food in one year.

33
00:02:03,492 --> 00:02:08,322
Work it through — that is about eight hundred and forty-seven dollars a month,

34
00:02:08,322 --> 00:02:10,179
walked out of a store in bags.

35
00:02:10,980 --> 00:02:12,175
Divided by four percent,

36
00:02:12,175 --> 00:02:16,257
the grocery bill needs two hundred fifty-four thousand dollars standing behind it.

37
00:02:17,057 --> 00:02:21,064
Two hundred fifty-four thousand dollars, drawn at four percent a year,

38
00:02:21,064 --> 00:02:23,640
and the grocery bill stops costing you hours.

39
00:02:24,439 --> 00:02:28,651
That is rung one. Notice what changed: the bill is the same,

40
00:02:28,651 --> 00:02:31,388
but your hours are no longer paying it.

41
00:02:32,188 --> 00:02:34,278
Rung two is parked outside your building.

42
00:02:35,078 --> 00:02:37,232
The same survey puts transportation at

43
00:02:37,232 --> 00:02:40,407
thirteen thousand three hundred eighteen dollars a year.

44
00:02:41,207 --> 00:02:45,594
Work it through — that is about eleven hundred and ten dollars a month,

45
00:02:45,594 --> 00:02:48,312
all of it: payment, insurance, gas, repairs.

46
00:02:49,112 --> 00:02:50,355
Divided by four percent,

47
00:02:50,355 --> 00:02:52,607
the car and everything it costs needs about

48
00:02:52,607 --> 00:02:54,911
three hundred thirty-three thousand dollars.

49
00:02:55,711 --> 00:02:58,686
Rung three is the biggest line in the American budget,

50
00:02:58,686 --> 00:03:01,275
and it has a landlord or a bank attached to it.

51
00:03:02,075 --> 00:03:04,688
Housing came to twenty-six thousand two hundred

52
00:03:04,688 --> 00:03:07,691
sixty-six dollars for the average household that year.

53
00:03:08,492 --> 00:03:10,799
That is a third of everything they spent,

54
00:03:10,799 --> 00:03:14,343
and about two thousand one hundred eighty-nine dollars a month.

55
00:03:15,143 --> 00:03:16,432
Divided by four percent,

56
00:03:16,432 --> 00:03:20,942
rent or the mortgage needs about six hundred fifty-seven thousand dollars behind it.

57
00:03:21,742 --> 00:03:24,383
Say that one slowly, because it is where the number

58
00:03:24,383 --> 00:03:27,541
stops sounding like savings and starts sounding like a house.

59
00:03:28,341 --> 00:03:31,110
Now understand what those papers say about themselves.

60
00:03:31,910 --> 00:03:34,930
The Trinity study states in one sentence that it

61
00:03:34,930 --> 00:03:38,075
did not adjust for taxes or for transaction costs.

62
00:03:38,875 --> 00:03:41,890
So every rung on this ladder is a before-tax number,

63
00:03:41,890 --> 00:03:44,674
and your real one is bigger than the arithmetic.

64
00:03:45,474 --> 00:03:47,311
The second limit is the clock,

65
00:03:47,311 --> 00:03:50,986
because both papers were built for a thirty-year retirement.

66
00:03:51,786 --> 00:03:54,661
Trinity's own conclusion says early retirees who

67
00:03:54,661 --> 00:03:58,134
expect long payouts should plan on lower withdrawal rates.

68
00:03:58,934 --> 00:04:03,273
That is the only sourced sentence about stopping work early in this video,

69
00:04:03,273 --> 00:04:04,446
and it is a warning.

70
00:04:05,246 --> 00:04:07,989
Which brings up the number you actually came here for.

71
00:04:08,789 --> 00:04:12,321
Five thousand dollars a month is sixty thousand dollars a year,

72
00:04:12,321 --> 00:04:15,293
and it sits deliberately below the average household.

73
00:04:16,093 --> 00:04:19,210
Work it through — sixty thousand dollars a year,

74
00:04:19,210 --> 00:04:23,042
divided by four percent, is one and a half million dollars.

75
00:04:23,842 --> 00:04:27,621
One and a half million dollars, at a four percent withdrawal rate,

76
00:04:27,621 --> 00:04:29,511
is five thousand dollars a month.

77
00:04:30,310 --> 00:04:33,063
Notice what that is. It is division —

78
00:04:33,063 --> 00:04:36,710
not a forecast, not a market call, not a promise.

79
00:04:37,510 --> 00:04:42,421
Now the warning, and it is the reason most people never reach any of these rungs.

80
00:04:43,221 --> 00:04:46,999
Somewhere on your feed there is a payout advertised at ten percent,

81
00:04:46,999 --> 00:04:49,255
or twelve, and it looks like a shortcut.

82
00:04:50,056 --> 00:04:54,000
Go back to the tank, because twelve percent is a promise about the tap.

83
00:04:54,800 --> 00:04:58,110
The papers tested how long a tank lasts at each rate,

84
00:04:58,110 --> 00:05:00,233
and no advertisement changes that.

85
00:05:01,034 --> 00:05:03,432
The number on the screen is usually real,

86
00:05:03,432 --> 00:05:06,180
because a yield is a payout divided by a price.

87
00:05:06,980 --> 00:05:10,592
So a yield can double without one extra dollar being paid out,

88
00:05:10,592 --> 00:05:13,563
because the price fell and the tank shrank with it.

89
00:05:14,363 --> 00:05:16,292
Going back to eighteen seventy-one,

90
00:05:16,292 --> 00:05:19,804
the highest reading ever was thirteen point eight four percent,

91
00:05:19,804 --> 00:05:21,364
in June nineteen thirty-two.

92
00:05:22,163 --> 00:05:24,970
That was the bottom of the Great Depression,

93
00:05:24,970 --> 00:05:28,798
and the yield was enormous because the prices had collapsed.

94
00:05:29,599 --> 00:05:32,901
So a very big yield is very often a very small price,

95
00:05:32,901 --> 00:05:35,268
and a smaller price is a smaller tank.

96
00:05:36,067 --> 00:05:38,656
Which leaves the question in the title. What

97
00:05:38,656 --> 00:05:41,363
if you only spend what the portfolio pays you,

98
00:05:41,363 --> 00:05:42,702
and never sell a share?

99
00:05:43,502 --> 00:05:48,413
That is the dividends-only route, and it is a real strategy with a real price tag.

100
00:05:49,213 --> 00:05:52,975
Right now the S and P five hundred's dividend yield is about one percent.

101
00:05:53,775 --> 00:05:56,137
One tracker reads one point zero four,

102
00:05:56,137 --> 00:06:00,488
another reads one point zero eight, and July was the lowest on record.

103
00:06:01,288 --> 00:06:03,387
Think of it this way — same tank,

104
00:06:03,387 --> 00:06:08,158
but now you may only take what it hands you, and that is about one percent.

105
00:06:08,959 --> 00:06:13,374
So run the same division at one percent, and watch what happens to the number.

106
00:06:14,173 --> 00:06:18,692
That same five thousand a month, from index dividends alone at about one percent,

107
00:06:18,692 --> 00:06:20,756
needs five point six million dollars.

108
00:06:21,556 --> 00:06:23,853
One and a half million at four percent,

109
00:06:23,853 --> 00:06:28,269
or five point six million at about one percent, for the identical paycheck.

110
00:06:29,070 --> 00:06:31,680
Notice what changed: nothing but the rate,

111
00:06:31,680 --> 00:06:35,470
and the same paycheck now costs roughly four times the money.

112
00:06:36,270 --> 00:06:40,200
Funds built around high-dividend stocks pay more than the index does,

113
00:06:40,200 --> 00:06:41,965
at roughly three percent today.

114
00:06:42,764 --> 00:06:44,155
At about three percent,

115
00:06:44,155 --> 00:06:48,328
that five thousand a month lands near one point nine million dollars.

116
00:06:49,128 --> 00:06:51,525
Three routes, three rates, one paycheck,

117
00:06:51,525 --> 00:06:55,241
and the rate you assume is the entire difference between them.

118
00:06:56,041 --> 00:06:59,150
Rung five is the whole month, not one slice of it.

119
00:06:59,950 --> 00:07:02,793
The average American household spent seventy-eight

120
00:07:02,793 --> 00:07:05,749
thousand five hundred thirty-five dollars that year.

121
00:07:06,549 --> 00:07:10,857
Work it through — that is six thousand five hundred forty-five a month,

122
00:07:10,857 --> 00:07:14,255
and at four percent it needs one point nine six million.

123
00:07:15,055 --> 00:07:18,077
The same whole month from index dividends alone,

124
00:07:18,077 --> 00:07:21,351
at about one percent, is over seven million dollars.

125
00:07:22,150 --> 00:07:24,202
One more thing about the four percent,

126
00:07:24,202 --> 00:07:27,819
because it is not a settled number and nobody should pretend it is.

127
00:07:28,619 --> 00:07:31,427
Morningstar puts the safe starting rate at three point

128
00:07:31,427 --> 00:07:34,288
nine percent for someone retiring in twenty twenty-six.

129
00:07:35,088 --> 00:07:38,755
Bengen himself moved his own number up to four point seven percent,

130
00:07:38,755 --> 00:07:41,436
in a book published in August twenty twenty-five.

131
00:07:42,235 --> 00:07:44,559
And a two thousand ten study found the four

132
00:07:44,559 --> 00:07:47,747
percent rule did not hold up across most developed markets.

133
00:07:48,547 --> 00:07:49,892
Same rule, three answers,

134
00:07:49,892 --> 00:07:53,928
which is exactly why the rate belongs next to the number every single time.

135
00:07:54,728 --> 00:07:57,915
So here is the ladder in order, all of it at four percent.

136
00:07:58,715 --> 00:08:02,076
At four percent, groceries are a quarter of a million. The car,

137
00:08:02,076 --> 00:08:05,011
a third of a million. Housing, two thirds of a million.

138
00:08:05,811 --> 00:08:08,616
Both at four percent. Five thousand a month,

139
00:08:08,616 --> 00:08:13,334
one and a half million. The average household, one point nine six million.

140
00:08:14,134 --> 00:08:18,095
And that same five thousand a month from index dividends alone,

141
00:08:18,095 --> 00:08:21,239
at about one percent, near five point six million.

142
00:08:22,039 --> 00:08:23,865
Every one of those is a division,

143
00:08:23,865 --> 00:08:27,185
and every one is only as good as the rate sitting beside it.

144
00:08:27,986 --> 00:08:31,207
Now go back to the day this started on. No alarm,

145
00:08:31,207 --> 00:08:34,099
no call from work, and one buzz before noon.

146
00:08:34,898 --> 00:08:37,082
That number does not buy a lifestyle. It

147
00:08:37,082 --> 00:08:39,757
buys back the hours that used to go to the bills.

148
00:08:40,557 --> 00:08:43,431
If the number is less mysterious now, subscribe,

149
00:08:43,431 --> 00:08:47,323
because the next one prices the rungs nobody puts on a thumbnail.
